00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:24,000
No.

183
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:25,000
For sure.

184
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:26,000
Yeah.

185
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,920
That's one thing I don't know if we ever had this conversation when you were like looking

186
00:11:28,920 --> 00:11:33,800
to come fly, is it like all you have to do is I'm like oversimplifying here, but like

187
00:11:33,800 --> 00:11:39,440
to get into a flight program is you have to demonstrate that you're like not an asshole

188
00:11:39,440 --> 00:11:40,440
and that you're trainable.

189
00:11:40,440 --> 00:11:43,680
Like, and then you get along with people and they will teach you the things that you

190
00:11:43,680 --> 00:11:44,680
don't know yet.

191
00:11:44,680 --> 00:11:49,960
So like expecting people to know how to operate in an aircraft when they never done it is,

192
00:11:49,960 --> 00:11:50,960
is unfair.

193
00:11:50,960 --> 00:11:55,160
Like expecting someone to know how to like take care of like a sick OB patient or a kid

194
00:11:55,160 --> 00:11:56,160
and they never done it.

195
00:11:56,160 --> 00:11:57,880
And that's, that's unrealistic.

196
00:11:57,880 --> 00:11:59,720
So yeah, I think.

197
00:11:59,720 --> 00:12:05,280
And also, I mean, even when you train, you know, and I think we're going to get to this,

198
00:12:06,160 --> 00:12:13,480
I got lucky, I think in that I had a lot of time to train in a rotor, like during my orientation.

199
00:12:13,480 --> 00:12:14,480
Yeah.

200
00:12:14,480 --> 00:12:15,920
But like some people don't get that at all.

201
00:12:15,920 --> 00:12:20,200
Like they're training on fixed wing and then they're coming to a rotor base and they're

202
00:12:20,200 --> 00:12:23,080
like, well, I've never even flown in a helicopter.

203
00:12:23,080 --> 00:12:25,360
Like how do you expect me to work now?

204
00:12:25,360 --> 00:12:27,200
Yeah, that's how it was for me.

205
00:12:27,200 --> 00:12:32,720
Because when I started here, we didn't have any, we only had eight stars in the state.

206
00:12:32,720 --> 00:12:34,920
So all of my orientation was a fixed wing.

207
00:12:34,920 --> 00:12:39,560
And then like your first rotor shift is like the first time you're like putting on your

208
00:12:39,560 --> 00:12:41,440
helmet in the GZ going to a scene flight.

209
00:12:41,440 --> 00:12:47,120
And like, I was lucky that I had some background in like the side, but I can't imagine like

210
00:12:47,120 --> 00:12:52,320
coming into that scene, like not having done that, like trying to manage your first scene.

211
00:12:52,320 --> 00:12:53,320
Yeah.

212
00:12:53,320 --> 00:12:54,320
Without.

213
00:12:54,320 --> 00:12:55,320
Yeah.

214
00:12:55,320 --> 00:12:56,320
Yeah.

215
00:12:56,320 --> 00:13:01,520
And that's, I think that's what I came to realize is like the more challenging part

216
00:13:01,520 --> 00:13:10,680
of, of air medical is, is like the, the stuff that you just have no exposure to.

217
00:13:10,680 --> 00:13:11,680
Right?

218
00:13:11,680 --> 00:13:17,280
Like, like when I went on my first scene call, luckily I was with somebody that knew what

219
00:13:17,280 --> 00:13:18,280
they were doing.

220
00:13:18,280 --> 00:13:22,720
Cause I was just like this lost puppy, like wandering around like, yeah, I can carry that.

221
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:23,720
I can do that.

222
00:13:23,720 --> 00:13:24,720
Or like, sure.

223
00:13:24,720 --> 00:13:25,720
Sure.

224
00:13:25,720 --> 00:13:26,720
Yeah.

225
00:13:26,720 --> 00:13:31,000
Like whatever totally just like running around, not really being efficient in any way.

226
00:13:31,480 --> 00:13:35,200
But I think it's also worth remembering that like, that's fine.

227
00:13:35,200 --> 00:13:36,200
Like, yeah.

228
00:13:36,200 --> 00:13:37,200
Yeah.

229
00:13:37,200 --> 00:13:41,800
I think like we tend to have this perception, like we as like Brownian as people and like

230
00:13:41,800 --> 00:13:45,880
nurse in the hospital that like the flight crew is like, yeah, they're on it all the

231
00:13:45,880 --> 00:13:46,880
time.

232
00:13:46,880 --> 00:13:47,880
Yeah.

233
00:13:47,880 --> 00:13:48,880
Now they just wear cool pajamas.

234
00:13:48,880 --> 00:13:49,880
Yeah.

235
00:13:49,880 --> 00:13:50,880
That's it.

236
00:13:50,880 --> 00:13:51,880
Yeah.

237
00:13:51,880 --> 00:13:55,560
No, it has been an interesting experience to see that firsthand too, where, you know,

238
00:13:55,560 --> 00:14:01,280
I walk into a room and I'm like, you know, shitting my pants, but what we're about to

239
00:14:01,280 --> 00:14:05,000
do and like, what's going to happen and everybody's looking at you like, okay, what's next?

240
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
I don't know.

241
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:08,000
Yeah.

242
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:09,000
Yeah.

243
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:13,060
And sometimes you just got to like do something or like convince everything to everyone else

244
00:14:13,060 --> 00:14:15,920
to do nothing, you know, just like make a decision one way or the other.

245
00:14:15,920 --> 00:14:16,920
Yeah.

246
00:14:16,920 --> 00:14:20,760
You don't necessarily have to do stuff, but like voice a decision and it's probably going

247
00:14:20,760 --> 00:14:21,760
to be fine.

248
00:14:21,760 --> 00:14:27,920
So then like going back to this, you know, fake it till you make it concept, right?

249
00:14:27,920 --> 00:14:35,520
I think it's so, um, it's very useful and it can be pretty prevalent in critical care

250
00:14:35,520 --> 00:14:37,200
space in general.

251
00:14:37,200 --> 00:14:39,880
Um, and it does help.

252
00:14:39,880 --> 00:14:44,400
It totally helped me get from where I was to where I am.

